WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.557
What a serving engine does.

2
00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:10.264
When you ran a model locally in week 1, one request at a time, generation looked simple: tokenize, run the model, sample, repeat.

3
00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:13.168
A production server faces a different problem.

4
00:00:13.448 --> 00:00:22.387
Requests arrive at random times, with prompts and outputs of different lengths, and you are paying for the GPU every minute whether it is busy or not.

5
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:29.557
The serving engine’s job is to keep the GPU doing useful work while keeping each request’s wait acceptable.

6
00:00:29.837 --> 00:00:31.075
Two phases matter.

7
00:00:31.355 --> 00:00:40.485
Prefill processes the whole prompt in one pass and is compute-heavy: a 600-token prompt becomes a large matrix multiplication.

8
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:51.368
Decode then produces output tokens one at a time, and each step must read essentially all of the model’s weights from GPU memory to produce a single token per sequence.

9
00:00:51.648 --> 00:01:06.347
Decode is limited by memory bandwidth, not arithmetic, which is the key to everything that follows: if each step reads the weights once anyway, producing a token for 32 sequences in that step costs little more than producing one.

10
00:01:06.627 --> 00:01:15.437
During decode the model also keeps a KV cache: the attention keys and values for every token so far, so it doesn’t recompute them each step.

11
00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:23.227
The KV cache grows with sequence length and with the number of sequences in flight, and it competes with the weights for GPU memory.

12
00:01:23.507 --> 00:01:28.371
How many requests you can serve at once is often set by how much KV cache fits.

13
00:01:28.651 --> 00:01:31.317
For your extraction task this shape is favorable.

14
00:01:31.597 --> 00:01:39.939
Prompts are short once the model is fine-tuned (no long instructions or few-shot examples), and outputs are a small JSON object.

15
00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:46.853
Short prompts and short outputs mean many sequences fit in memory at once, which is exactly where batching pays.
